{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/249032-i-really-like-all-the-post-forge-stuff-in-demon-and-through-it-gmc","id":249032,"title":"I really like all the post-Forge stuff in Demon (and through it, GMC)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-04T11:56:52-07:00","id":249032,"title":"I really like all the post-Forge stuff in Demon (and through it, GMC)","content":"Here's some stuff I love about Demon the Descent (and through it, GMC) that makes it a really terrific game:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>It has very clear stuff for the GM and players to DO. This is a very &quot;postmodern&quot; gaming thing. The Apocalypse World engine, for example, gives players and GM's a list of things they can do, making it very clear what you do in a game session or scene. Similarly, Demon gives me very clear things to do as a GM (create cipher, run multiple Infrastructure plots with 4 stages, etc.). Players similarly have stuff to do (monitor infrastructure, pursue ambitions, learn about their cipher)</li> <li>The separation of &quot;cool character development being dovetailed by mechanics&quot; is very postmodern. I love that it separates &quot;your ability to get more awesome through setbacks&quot; (beats, conditions that generate beats) from &quot;being your character makes you more awesome&quot; (Willpower).</li> <li>The doors mechanic for social stuff has to be on your short-list of best social mechanics.</li> <li>The very simple but still fun dice pool mechanic.</li> <li>The unified spirit mechanic. If you combine this with the last antagonist section from Mortal Remains, you have an awesome way to create antagonists without using all of the same heavy-lifting required for characters. Again, very postmodern rpg.</li> </ol>Just wanted to mention these things. What are yours?","signature":""